<h2>The dispute: an unfinished hotel, an unpaid contractor</h2>
<p>Voltas carried out HVAC and electrical installation for the hotel project under a work order amended repeatedly as the project evolved. The relationship broke down over completion, the final bill, retention money, completion of balance works through third parties and statutory electrical clearance. A court-appointed sole arbitrator worked through the claims and counter-claims: the hotel&rsquo;s counter-claims fell to limitation; the contractor&rsquo;s claims survived it; the breach and abandonment issues went to the contractor, with credit to the hotel for its third-party completion costs; and the valuation adopted was the surveyor&rsquo;s — undisputed on the record.</p>

<h2>The Section 34 challenge, and its one success</h2>
<p>The hotel&rsquo;s attack on the main award failed at the threshold of the modern public-policy framework. Applying <em>Associate Builders v. DDA</em>, <em>Dyna Technologies v. Crompton Greaves</em> and <em>OPG Power Generation v. Enexio</em>, the Court found the award internally consistent, reasoned and anchored in the material — and reiterated that Section 34 is not an appellate rehearing of evidence.</p>
<p>The exception was structural, not evaluative. The retention-money head of Rs 1,01,53,833 had been awarded although Voltas did not press that claim. An award on a claim not pressed is not a finding a review court must defer to; it is a discrete excess. The question was remedial: can the court cut out that head without disturbing the rest?</p>

<h2>Severability after Gayatri Balasamy</h2>
<div class="compare">
  <div class="col old">
    <h3>The older anxiety</h3>
    <p>Courts hesitated between two extremes — upholding a flawed award whole, or setting the entire award aside and sending years of arbitration back to zero over one defective head.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="arrow">&rarr;</div>
  <div class="col new">
    <h3>The current position</h3>
    <p>The Constitution Bench in <em>Gayatri Balasamy v. ISG Novasoft</em> recognised that a severable, self-contained portion of an award can be set aside alone, leaving the valid remainder enforceable.</p>
  </div>
</div>
<p>This decision applies that doctrine in a particularly clean setting: the severed head was not intertwined with the balance-work valuation, rested on no pressed claim, and could be excised arithmetically. The result preserves four years of adjudication while removing precisely the rupees that should never have been awarded.</p>

<h2>Practice notes</h2>
<div class="check">
  <p>Keep a contemporaneous record of which claims are pressed, modified or given up before the tribunal — the written submissions are the audit trail on which a severance argument later stands or falls.</p>
  <p>In Section 34 petitions, plead severance in the alternative wherever the challenge targets discrete heads; a prayer aimed only at wholesale setting aside invites all-or-nothing outcomes.</p>
  <p>Respondents defending awards should concede severable excesses early; saving the core award is worth more than defending an indefensible head.</p>
  <p>Interest architecture — pre-reference, pendente lite, post-award — is tribunal discretion under Section 31(7); challenges to rate alone rarely justify the petition that carries them.</p>
</div>
<div class="note">The judgment also records the familiar institutional lesson of construction arbitration: the award of 1 March 2019 took until late 2026 to clear one tier of challenge. Clients should plan enforcement timelines — and settlement positions — on that reality rather than on the statute&rsquo;s optimistic calendar.</div>
